"A ruler gives a more precise measurement than your hand.": "e9928d55cebe",
"Astronomers use the same trick, but with two eyes much farther apart. Every six months, Earth reaches the opposite side of its orbit, about 300 million kilometers from where it was. Seen from these two places, a nearby star shifts a tiny bit against much more distant stars in the background. Measure the angle of that shift, draw a triangle, and you can work out the distance. The angle is tiny: even for Proxima Centauri, it is like the width of a coin seen from more than five kilometers away. Farther stars shift even less.": "e276cfd1be48",
"Because light needs time to travel, looking at the stars means looking into the past. The light from Sirius that reaches you tonight left it about eight and a half years ago. Vega's light left 25 years ago, and Betelgeuse's light began its journey around 500 years ago, during China's Ming dynasty. The poet Li Bai wrote that people today cannot see the Moon of ancient times, yet this same Moon once shone on the ancients. Starlight turns his idea around: tonight you can see light that set out while people of the past were alive.": "4d060cd0f7ff",
"For more than 2,000 years, people looked for this shift and could not find it. Some ancient Greek thinkers even took this as proof that Earth does not move. The shift was simply too small for their eyes. At last, in 1838, the German astronomer Friedrich Bessel measured the parallax of a faint star called 61 Cygni and found it was about 10 light-years away. Today we know it is 11.4. In 1989, the European satellite Hipparcos began making precise measurements from above the blurry air, for about 118,000 stars, and its successor, Gaia, has measured nearly 1.5 billion.": "8d8f979a6afa",
"How can you measure the distance to something you can never visit? Try this. Hold up your thumb at arm's length and look at it with one eye. Then blink and look with the other eye. Your thumb seems to jump against the background. This jump is called parallax. Bring your thumb closer to your face, and it jumps more; move it farther away, and it jumps less. Your brain uses the small difference between your two eyes to judge how far away things are.": "28ef76016811",
"In the photo, my sister is smiling with a mountain in the background.": "85edd53d6654",
"Look up on a clear night, and all the stars seem to sit on the same dark dome, as if they were the same distance away. They are not. Some stars are close neighbors of the Sun, and others are hundreds of times farther. The distances are so huge that kilometers are no help, so astronomers use the light-year: the distance light travels in one year, about 9.46 trillion kilometers. A light-year is a distance, not a time. Proxima Centauri, the nearest star after the Sun, is about 4.2 light-years away.": "0e2a6277a0aa",
"Look up on a clear night, and all the stars seem to sit on the same dark dome, as if they were the same distance away. They are not. Some stars are close neighbors of the Sun, and others are hundreds of times farther. The distances are so huge that kilometers are no help, so astronomers use the light-year: the distance light travels in one year, about 9.46 trillion kilometers. A light-year is a distance, not a time. Proxima Centauri, the nearest star after the Sun, is about 4.2 light-years away. How can you measure the distance to something you can never visit? Try this. Hold up your thumb at arm's length and look at it with one eye. Then blink and look with the other eye. Your thumb seems to jump against the background. This jump is called parallax. Bring your thumb closer to your face, and it jumps more; move it farther away, and it jumps less. Your brain uses the small difference between your two eyes to judge how far away things are. Astronomers use the same trick, but with two eyes much farther apart. Every six months, Earth reaches the opposite side of its orbit, about 300 million kilometers from where it was. Seen from these two places, a nearby star shifts a tiny bit against much more distant stars in the background. Measure the angle of that shift, draw a triangle, and you can work out the distance. The angle is tiny: even for Proxima Centauri, it is like the width of a coin seen from more than five kilometers away. Farther stars shift even less. For more than 2,000 years, people looked for this shift and could not find it. Some ancient Greek thinkers even took this as proof that Earth does not move. The shift was simply too small for their eyes. At last, in 1838, the German astronomer Friedrich Bessel measured the parallax of a faint star called 61 Cygni and found it was about 10 light-years away. Today we know it is 11.4. In 1989, the European satellite Hipparcos began making precise measurements from above the blurry air, for about 118,000 stars, and its successor, Gaia, has measured nearly 1.5 billion. Because light needs time to travel, looking at the stars means looking into the past. The light from Sirius that reaches you tonight left it about eight and a half years ago. Vega's light left 25 years ago, and Betelgeuse's light began its journey around 500 years ago, during China's Ming dynasty. The poet Li Bai wrote that people today cannot see the Moon of ancient times, yet this same Moon once shone on the ancients. Starlight turns his idea around: tonight you can see light that set out while people of the past were alive. Parallax is the first step on a ladder of distances. It works well for nearby stars, but for very distant ones the shift is too small to measure well, so astronomers use other clues, such as how bright a star really is. Even some famous bright stars, like Deneb, still have uncertain distances. Use the list below to find out when tonight's starlight left home, and look for your own birthday star: a star whose light set out in the year you were born.": "0f8459c38aed",
